**Common quantum-foundation inspection — 1 November 2026:** rechecked the author's current page and authorized 133-page PDF. Read selected actual text in section 1.1 (printed 3–4), section 1.3 (16–17), section 2.1.2 (24–26), section 3.3 and 3.3.1 (76–80), and section 3.4.1 (84–86, through the positive-norm uncertainty argument). The common block reconstructs the ideal well and adds an original two-mode comparison. Source figures are referenced, not copied or treated as newly analyzed datasets. The larger reading assignment, external-media boundary and rights statement remain unchanged.

The common uncertainty lesson qualifies the printed-page-85 statement about noncommuting observables: a nonzero commutator does not generally preclude a common eigenstate. An explicit three-coordinate example has a nonzero commutator and both variances zero in a shared eigenstate. The valid bound uses the commutator's expectation in the particular state. Similarly, the page-78 general language about measurement disturbance needs the stated preparation/instrument context: an ideal projective measurement can leave its eigenstate unchanged. These finite counterexamples and the common projective-instrument assumptions prevent overextending the source's informal language.

